{"cluster_id": "C-1922", "topic": "Interpreters SystemLog h registers the ContentAddressedGarbageCollectionLog", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["B051-077"], "canonical_claim": "`Interpreters/SystemLog.h` registers the `ContentAddressedGarbageCollectionLog` and `ContentAddressedLog` system logs.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#system-log-h"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1923", "topic": "The parser and AST support CONTENT_ADDRESSED_GARBAGE_COLLECTION", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["B051-079"], "canonical_claim": "The parser and AST support `CONTENT_ADDRESSED_GARBAGE_COLLECTION`, `CONTENT_ADDRESSED_GC_REBUILD`, and `CONTENT_ADDRESSED_DROP_POOL_MEMBER`, including the `content_addressed_gc_rebuild_force` field and round-trip parser coverage.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#parsers-system-query"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1924", "topic": "The magic_enum enum_range for ASTSystemQuery Type", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["B051-080"], "canonical_claim": "The `magic_enum::enum_range` for `ASTSystemQuery::Type` must be widened to `[0,255]` because the added CAS `SYSTEM` query types exceed the default range.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#parsers-system-query"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1925", "topic": "A build heartbeat is durable in", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-012"], "canonical_claim": "A build heartbeat is durable in the pool before the first blob PUT via the pool-wide gc/hb pulse plus the writer's mount object build-watermark floor; there is no separate builds/<build_id> object in the current layout.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#write-path-overview"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1926", "topic": "Regular GC deletion outcomes are classified", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-016"], "canonical_claim": "Regular GC deletion outcomes are classified as deleted | absent | replaced (412 = resurrection won) | spared, and retire entries are dropped only on confirmed outcomes.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#regular-gc"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1927", "topic": "resurrect-reupload token handling", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-051", "B007-077", "B007-085", "B035-028"], "canonical_claim": "The force-Read override HEADs only fire from `closeBlob` on hashes touched in the current fold window (`cur_edges == 0`); `settleRetiredBelow`'s quiescent-entry path never issues a HEAD and must not be changed to do so. The resurrect-reupload fix keys blob condemnation on `(hash, current token)` instead of only on the hash, and re-condemns a current token when a touched hash has net in-degree zero and a prior retired token differs. The resurrect-reupload fix makes `closeBlob` HEAD the current token when a prior retired entry reaches zero in-degree and condemns the current token if it differs from the retired token, matching `CaIncarnationCore`'s `GRetire` behavior. The resurrect-replaced repair adds one HEAD only for a touched condemned transition; quiescent graduation through `settleRetiredBelow` takes no additional HEAD.", "issue_ids": ["S30"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-07-cas-resurrect-reupload-orphan-fix.md#global-constraints", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-07-cas-resurrect-reupload-orphan-fix.md#goal", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-07-cas-resurrect-reupload-orphan-fix.md#task-3", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-07-cas-resurrect-reupload-orphan-fix-design.md#cost", "utils/ca-soak/scenarios/BACKLOG.md#L1209-L1249"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1928", "topic": "On a resurrect supersede the fold", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-052"], "canonical_claim": "On a resurrect supersede the fold keeps exactly one retired entry per hash: the stale entry is dropped, not kept alongside the fresh re-condemn, and the retired list stays hash-sorted.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-07-cas-resurrect-reupload-orphan-fix.md#global-constraints"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1929", "topic": "Sharded GC correctness was validated by", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-076"], "canonical_claim": "Sharded-GC correctness was validated by S15: identical oracle checksums and `dangling=0` under both `gc_shards=1` (default) and `gc_shards=2`, with reducer memory flat (0.60->0.61 GB) and orphan backlog fully drained in both configurations.", "issue_ids": ["S15"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-06-scenario-validation-night.md#s15"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1930", "topic": "The frontier round s universe is", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-094"], "canonical_claim": "The frontier round's \"universe\" is exactly every `Live`/`Removing` row of the round's own frozen catalog cut and nothing else; an older comment defining it as \"hint \u222a sealed cursors \u222a catalog Live/Removing entries\" is stale and contradicts the code, which implements only the catalog-cut definition.", "issue_ids": ["P-2"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/t6-review-fable.md#L155-L159"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1931", "topic": "GC s raw baseline rebuild condemns", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-028"], "canonical_claim": "GC's raw baseline rebuild condemns nothing: it only rebuilds cursors and edges. An earlier version ended the pass by LISTing `blobs/` and condemning every physically-present body outside the rebuilt edge set, but this was found unsafe (r5-finding-4) because both legs of the traversal (owner replay and the trimmed-but-live manifest pass) are listing-driven, so a store that omits a durable key from one enumeration hides a live owner while the same pass condemns the blob that owner pins \u2014 an observed real failure mode (the `0x1430c` shape), not a hypothetical.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#L551-L560"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1932", "topic": "Patch parts lightweight deletes are first", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-031"], "canonical_claim": "Patch parts / lightweight deletes are first-class GC reachability roots: a patch manifest references the base part's patched data columns plus its own delta blobs, and while a patch ref is active it transitively keeps alive both its own delta blobs and the base blobs it references, even if the base part is otherwise superseded, provided the MARK walk treats patch manifests as roots. When a patch is materialized into the base via merge, the patch ref is released and its uniquely-owned delta blobs become sweepable. Patch-on-content-addressed-base is flagged as not yet fully model-checked in the v3 design's residual-risk list.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#L450-L459"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1933", "topic": "A stateless ref less reader that", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-032"], "canonical_claim": "A stateless/ref-less reader that attaches by reading the catalog and registers no `/parts` ref is invisible to the per-process `use_count()==1` MARK-union fence, which is a valid cross-node fence only for replicas holding a `/parts` ref; owning replicas can release refs after `old_parts_lifetime` (480s) mid-SELECT, sweeping the manifest/blobs out from under the read. The preferred fix is an ephemeral reader pin (a Keeper node or equivalent) created at query start naming the parts/snapshot read, auto-released on session end/crash; this is distinct from the lost-replica timeout, which is a genuine leak needing a separate long-dead-replica reaping rule.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/04-gc-protocol.md#L461-L472"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1934", "topic": "clamp suppression", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-042", "B006-059"], "canonical_claim": "`CaGcAckFloorCore.tla`'s `SabotageClampNoSuppress` config, which removes the `~clampedL` graduation guard, reproduces the 2026-07-03 night incident of 31 dangling blobs as an `INV_NO_DANGLE` counterexample; the shipped clamp-suppression guard (`suppress_destructive` gating `Gc::graduationDue`, `CasGc.cpp:1360`) blocks graduation and pending deletes while any shard is clamped, so held refs persist in `landed` until a later clamp-free pass consumes them. If any fold pass records a clamp anomaly, destruction is suppressed: no graduations or redeletes occur, pending entries carry `UNCHANGED`, condemn/spare continue, and deletes resume on the first clamp-free pass; the pass emits a warning and increments `CasGcClampSuppressedPasses`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/06-tla-models.md#L951-L964", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-03-unattended-night.md#L75-L82"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1935", "topic": "putDeterministicArtifact is the write once helper", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-078"], "canonical_claim": "`putDeterministicArtifact` is the write-once helper for GC objects whose bytes are a pure function of their inputs (source-edge runs, fold seals): a `putIfAbsent` precondition failure is resolved by reading the existing object back and comparing bytes \u2014 byte-equal means the writer's own replay landed and is adopted as a no-op, while divergent bytes throw `CORRUPTED_DATA` as an impossible case. It is deliberately not used for observation-bearing artifacts like GC outcome logs, which use first-durable-write-wins byte-adopt semantics instead, and never for blob bodies because the fresh-tag rule makes two attempts of the same logical create legitimately differ.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#deterministic-artifacts"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1936", "topic": "In GC sharding a shard with", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-083"], "canonical_claim": "In GC sharding, a shard with an empty delta bucket and no condemned entries in the parent summary copies the parent's run references verbatim (\"pure carry\", zero run I/O); a missing parent summary entry on a non-fresh pool is `CORRUPTED_DATA`, never silently treated as zero.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#gc-sharding"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1937", "topic": "generation pruning", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-084", "B005-056"], "canonical_claim": "Generation pruning (GC phase 13) keeps the last `gc_snap_generations_to_keep` generations (default 3; 0 keeps everything for forensics) and deletes wholesale by LIST-and-delete-everything under the generation prefix, bounded to 64 generations per round. A generation still referenced by the live seal is skipped but the cursor still advances past it regardless, so leak-freedom depends entirely on the post-CAS hand-off reclaim in phase 14. Implemented retention skips at generation granularity and advances `snap_pruned_through` over every visited generation; when a reference moves off a skipped generation, hand-off deletes the whole `gc/gen/<g>/` prefix rather than one run, preventing leaks of the rest of that generation's artifacts.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#gc-pruning", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-snapshot-streaming-design.md#implementation-notes-landed-2026-07-02"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1938", "topic": "namespace removal cleanup", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-089", "AUDIT-M2-347", "B027-026", "B028-024", "B037-005", "B049-049"], "canonical_claim": "Namespace removal has no physical-empty handshake: the writer changes the catalog row from `Live` to `Removing`, appends the exact ref removals plus a `RemoveNamespace` op, and deletes nothing itself. GC's fold attaches cleanup evidence to that opaque life row, and only a later GC invocation's pre-fold drain exact-CAS-deletes the matching `Removing` catalog row before any successor plan can be published; the perpetual namespace janitor and orphan-manifest sweep reclaim the physical debris independently, and a same-name birth waits only for the catalog row to disappear. Namespace removal deletes lifecycle metadata only; it has no `suppress_destructive` input and performs no physical deletion, leaving `_ckpt`, stream objects, `_files`, and other dead-life objects to the perpetual `cas/ns/` janitor. Catalog deletion does not require physical emptiness: `_ckpt`, stream, `_snap`, and `_files` residue may remain for the perpetual janitor or a family-specific sweep. Lifecycle physical cleanup is not part of the pre-fold drain: `suppress_destructive` does not govern it, and the perpetual `cas/ns/` janitor plus orphan-manifest sweep own physical debris. Removal completion performs no namespace-owned physical LIST, physical delete, physical-empty proof, or second deletion window; `_ckpt`, stream, and namespace-file residue is leak-only janitor work, while orphan manifests remain the orphan sweep's work.; A dropped Task 5 test obligation, `NamespaceRemovalDoesNotListOrDeleteFiles`, was the executable proof that removal has no physical-empty dependency (no `_files` LIST, no `_files` delete, entry deleted while files still exist); it must be restored in any rewrite.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task-5-prefold-amendment-report.md#decisions-implemented", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#gc-sees-refs", "docs/superpowers/models/CaRefCatalogCore_RESULTS.md#L55-L58", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog.md#task-5", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-task5-critique-codex.md#silently-dropped-obligations-the-failure-mode-of-a-rewrite", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-27-cas-ref-chain-complete-cut-design.md#invariants"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1939", "topic": "Attribution of the night s 11", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-122"], "canonical_claim": "Attribution of the night's 11 concurrent GC clamps was: RustFS returned false 404s on HEAD during a metacache storm caused by the `rustfs#3231` overwrite-leak's directory bloat (00:37-00:55); the clamps rose with the storm and self-released when it subsided (~01:01), and the bodies the false 404s claimed were missing had never actually been deleted (no `tree_delete` for the pinned manifests). The full causal chain is: leak -> directory bloat -> metacache storm -> false 404s on HEAD -> GC clamps -> (pre-fix) graduation proceeding over unfolded landed events -> dangling blobs.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-03-unattended-night.md#L156-L164"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1940", "topic": "CAS probe A the ref scan", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-153", "AUDIT-M2-353", "AUDIT-M2-439", "B026-013", "B034-089"], "canonical_claim": "CAS probe A (the ref-scan disagreement detector) reports each hole with the ref-log id and its direction: holes missing from the fold's own (second) scan are explainable by concurrent deletion, but holes missing from the pre-fold (first) scan are not \u2014 the object existed at the second walk and its id sits below what the first walk had already observed, so the first walk should have returned it.; Probe A detects CAS ref-prefix LIST incompleteness via two independent enumerations per GC round (the pre-fold defer scan and fold's own enumeration); it compares per-namespace id sets and reports a hole only when the id is below the OTHER enumeration's own maximum for that namespace, which excludes ordinary in-flight appends between the two walks.; Probe A compares the two existing full enumerations of `cas/refs/` instead of performing another scan, so it adds a set comparison and no backend I/O.; The chosen interim response to the LIST-hole hypothesis was mechanism-agnostic detection: compare two ref-prefix enumerations before advancing a cursor and compare intended-to-fold records with actually applied records; the permanent journal-chain fix remains required before release.; In the CAS GC-observability skipped-transaction-detector design, probe A's independence from probe B rests on the two enumerations being separate physical LIST walks; if a future optimisation merges them (listing the same prefix twice is an obvious target), probe A would silently become vacuous, and the design leaves open whether the guard against this should be a code comment forbidding the merge or a test asserting two distinct LIST walks occur.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#list-as-journal-decision-c", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-25-cas-followups-detector-introspection-s42-forceclaim.md#task-2-probe-a-compare-the-two-enumerations-the-round-already-performs", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-26-list-incompleteness-investigation.md#L54-L67", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-25-cas-gc-observability-and-mount-force-design.md#item-1-open", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/CURRENT.md#L616-L631"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1941", "topic": "The Stage B strategic review concentration", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-209", "B031-004"], "canonical_claim": "The Stage B strategic-review concentration proposal's replacement namespace-removal sequence is: (1) catalog transitions Live to Removing; (2) the fenced owner appends the terminal record; (3) GC folds the terminal record and performs one bounded cleanup pass; (4) GC writes a stable retirement marker derived only from RootNamespace; (5) GC durably publishes a fold seal with that namespace's cursor pruned; (6) GC exact-deletes `_ckpt`; (7) catalog entry deletion happens last.; Task 5 must delete a catalog entry only after the cleanup marker and cleanup-item retirement are durable, and must prune the namespace cursor before deleting the entry.", "issue_ids": ["NEW-2"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-30-stage-b-critical-checkpoint-review.md#new-2-important-code-prose-an-un-cataloged-item-is-skipped-forever-and-the-suppression-is-permanent", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-codex.md#L82-L92"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1942", "topic": "Ordering the retirement marker before _ckpt", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-210"], "canonical_claim": "Ordering the retirement marker before `_ckpt` deletion means even catalog loss cannot turn a namespace-removal window into a fresh birth; creation must check both the retirement marker and the namespace's `_ckpt` by exact key before admission and refuse if either exists; a listing omission can leak old objects but cannot make a retired namespace visible again because it is never re-admitted.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-codex.md#L92"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1943", "topic": "Even under the no namespace reuse", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-211"], "canonical_claim": "Even under the no-namespace-reuse simplification, catalog authority, the `Creating` publication exclusion, the `Removing` positive-ownership exclusion, the exact-next frontier probe, durable holds, and the delete-site in-degree re-read must not be cut, because they prevent data loss independently of rebirth; a temporary \"refuse while old objects survive, retry after GC\" rule does not substitute for them since LIST cannot certify old-object absence.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-codex.md#L106"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1944", "topic": "C2 s widening past its three", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-223"], "canonical_claim": "C2's widening past its three originally-named sites is justified because `marker_key` is the guard that the physical manifest and verbatim passes consult, so they cannot run without a resolved life; the still-sentinel-keyed `namespaceFilesPrefix` is correct because every writer of namespace files is also sentinel-keyed, making that layer Task 6's responsibility rather than a C2 leftover.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-30-stage-b-critical-checkpoint-review.md#L108-L115"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1945", "topic": "The GC round s exact durable", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-259"], "canonical_claim": "The GC round's exact durable key layout: `gc/state` (controller state, one per pool, strict fail-closed CAGT-magic proto), `gc/hb` (advisory heartbeat, fixed 24-byte binary), `gc/gen/<g>/attempt/<a>/fold_seal` (write-once fold seal), `gc/gen/<g>/attempt/<a>/.../<round>/<shard>` (retire outcome log), `cas/refs/<ns>/_log/<txn_id>` (ref log txn), `cas/refs/<ns>/_snap/<id>.proto` (ref snapshot).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/03-gc-refs.md#2-1-persistent-gc-state-exact-keys-key-builders-live-in-caslayout-h"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1946", "topic": "source-edge reachability set", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-261", "B043-005"], "canonical_claim": "Blob reachability is source-edge set membership, not a counter: one source edge is (BlobRef, source_id) where source_id = CityHash128(namespace + ref + path), with 0 reserved as the sentinel key for kCondemned rows. The edge set is idempotent under re-fold, so retries and competing attempts produce byte-identical runs. Blob in-degree is derived from a folded set of source-edge deltas keyed by `(kind, hash, source_manifest)` rather than stored as a mutable counter; set semantics can delay reclamation but cannot accelerate deletion.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-source-edge", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/03-gc-refs.md#2-3-in-degree-accounting-model-confirmed"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1947", "topic": "Spared means a condemned blob s", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-262"], "canonical_claim": "\"Spared\" means a condemned blob's in-degree recovered because a publish re-referenced it; the retired entry is dropped and recovery wins even past the graduation floor. \"Forgotten\" has no live meaning in this subsystem \u2014 the memory-noted GForget/GcSnap::forget design is superseded by pruneSupersededGenerations (wholesale generation retention).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/03-gc-refs.md#2-4-what-condemned-retired-forgotten-mean-precisely-confirmed"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1948", "topic": "There is no crash resume step", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-267"], "canonical_claim": "There is no crash-resume step in the GC round: the whole round commits in the single `gc/state` CAS at the end, so a crashed pass leaves only attempt-scoped debris under its own unadopted lease.seq, never adopted and later reclaimed by `pruneSupersededGenerations` or the Task-7 hand-off. Every pre-CAS destructive action is justified by previously-published durable state (`delete_pending`), making replay under a fresh attempt idempotent.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/03-gc-refs.md#g-crash-resume-mid-round-confirmed"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1949", "topic": "The orphan manifest sweep gates deletion", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-270"], "canonical_claim": "The orphan-manifest sweep gates deletion by `prefixEligible`, a durable watermark-only fact (`min_active > build_sequence`, or the retired sentinel `min_active==UINT64_MAX`, or an older epoch; a missing watermark means NOT eligible), and skips any key still in `activeManifestKeys(ns)` (committed + live precommits + manifests removed above the durable fold cursor). It never GETs a condemned body to revive it.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/03-gc-refs.md#e-orphan-manifest-sweep-confirmed-casorphanmanifestsweep-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1950", "topic": "Stage B Task 5 s restated", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-300"], "canonical_claim": "Stage B Task 5's restated invariant: no same-name namespace entry can be created while any GC-held state (cursor, cleanup item, seal row) for the previous life survives; under this ordering, the catalog entry for a dropped table survives until the terminal record folds (a GC-round latency), so `CREATE TABLE` of a just-dropped name is refused \u2014 today as a `LOGICAL_ERROR` (`Pool/CasRefCatalog.cpp:236`) \u2014 until the next fold, which will burn stateless CI lanes that drop-and-recreate names constantly.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-fable.md#task5"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1951", "topic": "Capture at deposition recording NamespaceLifeId at", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-340"], "canonical_claim": "Capture-at-deposition (recording `NamespaceLifeId` at fold time rather than re-deriving it from the catalog) is mandatory because the stale-leader TLA+ sabotage shows that re-deriving the incarnation after rebirth targets the new life directly, causing data loss.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-task5-removalready-proposal.md#stale-cleanup-safety-without-cleanup"]}

